At 320, no overlap. Topbar fits. Good.

But 320 width, scene height 418, HUD top 422? Wait HUD inside scene y422-481 while scene bottom 491, yes. Pet bottom 398, tag to ~407, HUD top 422 gap 15. Good.

Now check landscape mobile 844x390 (height 390). Scene height? At width 844 >760 but <940: scene clamp(440,58vh=226,640)=440, plus topbar 62 + gap etc -> body scroll. It's okay; height 390 viewport, user scrolls. But maybe game not fully visible. Mobile landscape is less important but "Android and iOS phones and tablets". At 844x390, the page scrolls. The scene 440 + header 62 = 513 > 390; user must scroll to see HUD. Could be problematic for playing in landscape. We could add a landscape media query to reduce scene height to e.g. `calc(100dvh - 70px)` with min 300? At 844x390, a 320px scene would fit. Hmm. The CSS max-width 940 uses scene clamp min 440, causing overflow. For landscape phones (height < 500), maybe add `@media (max-height: 520px) and (orientation: landscape)` with `.scene { height: calc(100dvh - 76px); min-height: 300px; }` and maybe body overflow hidden, app height 100dvh. But sidebar below? Could keep scroll. The game itself would fit in viewport and upgrades below via scroll. This improves playability. But not essential. Let's consider.

At desktop 1280x720, scene 606? app height 720, top 88, shell height 720-88-? footer 26 + gap? shell bottom 682, height 594. So scene 594. fine.

At 844x390 landscape: with body overflow-y auto and app height auto, topbar 62, scene 440 -> HUD at y? top 73+440=513, outside viewport. Bad. Add landscape short rule:
```
@media (max-height: 520px) and (max-width: 940px) {
  body { overflow: hidden; }
